Ecrade has an integer array $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$. It's guaranteed that for each $1 \le i \lt n$, $a_i \neq a_{i+1}$. Ecrade can perform several operations on the array to make it strictly increasing. In each operation, he can choose two integers $l$ and $r$ ($1 \le l \le r \le n$) and replace $a_l, a_{l+1}, \ldots, a_r$ with any $r-l+1$ integers $a'_l, a'_{l+1}, \ldots, a'_r$ satisfying the following constraint: For each $l \le i \lt r$, the comparison between $a'_i$ and $a'_{i+1}$ is the same as that between $a_i$ and $a_{i+1}$, i.e., if $a_i \lt a_{i + 1}$, then $a'_i \lt a'_{i + 1}$; otherwise, if $a_i \gt a_{i + 1}$, then $a'_i \gt a'_{i + 1}$; otherwise, if $a_i = a_{i + 1}$, then $a'_i = a'_{i + 1}$. Ecrade wants to know the minimum number of operations to make the array strictly increasing. However, it seems a little difficult, so please help him!

